Let me re-read the slot allocation code precisely:

```rust
let slot_deadline = Instant::now() + watchdogs.slot_allocation_timeout;
let header_end = loop {
    ...
    let current_header_deadline = deadline.min(slot_deadline);
    let count = match read_checked(&mut stream, &mut buffer, cancellation, current_header_deadline, "reading response headers") {
        Ok(n) => n,
        Err(Error::ModelTransportTimedOut) if Instant::now() >= slot_deadline => {
            // → SlotAllocationTimedOut
        }
        Err(error) => return Err(error),
    };
    ...
};
```

So during header read (model loading), the read deadline is `min(deadline, slot_deadline)`. If the per-turn `deadline` is SHORTER than slot_deadline, then a timeout during loading reports as a generic `ModelTransportTimedOut` (not SlotAllocationTimedOut), and it fires at `deadline` (the per-turn deadline). So the loading IS capped by the per-turn deadline.

If the per-turn `deadline` is LONGER than slot_deadline, then the read deadline is slot_deadline, and a timeout there reports as SlotAllocationTimedOut, firing at slot_timeout.

So:
- If per-turn deadline < slot timeout: loading capped by per-turn deadline (bad — switch blocked by per-turn deadline).
- If per-turn deadline > slot timeout: loading capped by slot timeout (good — switch blocked by slot timeout).

So to make the switch covered by the slot timeout (decoupled from per-turn), we need per-turn deadline >= slot timeout. The user wants the per-turn deadline to NOT cover the switch, meaning they want slot timeout to be the binding constraint for loading, and per-turn deadline to apply only to the request after loading.

